From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 16:50:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drivers/elog: Option to answer no GSMI command from the OS

ELOG_GSMI gives two things: the SMI handler logs the entries into S3 and
S5, the wake sources and the power button on its own, and the OS can
send commands through the APMC port (APM_CNT_ELOG_GSMI): add an event,
clear the log, log an S0ix entry or exit. Linux's gsmi driver uses them
on Google boards. Elsewhere nothing does, while any code in ring 0 can
clear the event log of the firmware or fill it, and the handler reads
the parameters at the pointers the caller passes.

Add ELOG_GSMI_APMC, default y, so that nothing changes for the boards
that have it: without it gsmi_exec() answers every command as
unsupported, in one place for every SMI handler that calls it, and the
logging of the SMI handler stays.

TEST=Not built. With the option off, on a Lynx Point board: the event
log still has the S3 entry and the wake source of a suspend cycle, and
"outb 0xef, 0xb2" from the OS with the GSMI handshake in EAX returns
0x83 (unsupported) instead of 0x7f.
---
 src/drivers/elog/Kconfig | 14 ++++++++++++++
 src/drivers/elog/gsmi.c  |  4 ++++
 2 files changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/drivers/elog/Kconfig b/src/drivers/elog/Kconfig
index 19b33318..a26966b1 100644
--- a/src/drivers/elog/Kconfig
+++ b/src/drivers/elog/Kconfig
@@ -33,6 +33,20 @@ config ELOG_GSMI
 	  available with CONFIG_GOOGLE_GSMI and can be used to write
 	  kernel reset/shutdown messages to the event log.
 
+config ELOG_GSMI_APMC
+	bool "GSMI commands from the OS through the APMC port"
+	depends on ELOG_GSMI
+	default y
+	help
+	  Answer the GSMI commands that the OS sends to the SMI handler
+	  (APM_CNT_ELOG_GSMI): add an event, clear the log, log an S0ix
+	  entry or exit. The Linux driver (CONFIG_GOOGLE_GSMI) uses them on
+	  Google boards; any other code that runs in ring 0 can use them too,
+	  and clear the log or fill it. Without this option gsmi_exec() answers
+	  every command as unsupported, and the SMI handler keeps logging on
+	  its own: the entries into S3 and S5, the wake sources, the power
+	  button. Say N on a board whose OS has no use for the interface.
+
 config ELOG_BOOT_COUNT
 	bool "Maintain a monotonic boot number in CMOS"
 	default n
diff --git a/src/drivers/elog/gsmi.c b/src/drivers/elog/gsmi.c
index 36940f1c..2b17cc81 100644
--- a/src/drivers/elog/gsmi.c
+++ b/src/drivers/elog/gsmi.c
@@ -51,6 +51,10 @@ u32 gsmi_exec(u8 command, u32 *param)
 	struct gsmi_clear_eventlog_param *cel;
 	u32 ret = GSMI_RET_UNSUPPORTED;
 
+	/* The SMI handler logs on its own; the OS gets no command */
+	if (!CONFIG(ELOG_GSMI_APMC))
+		return ret;
+
 	switch (command) {
 	case GSMI_CMD_HANDSHAKE_TYPE:
 		/* Used by kernel to verify basic SMI functionality */
-- 
